<section xml:id="section-polynomial-rings">
  <title>Polynomial Rings</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-polynomial-rings-definition">
    <title>Definition</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="example-poly-ring"/>, I mentioned that polynomials
      can be made out of any commutative ring. Let me expand on that
      with a restated complete definition of a polynomial ring. (As
      mentioned in the last section, for this and the rest of our
      tour of ring theory, I will be assuming commutativity
      throughout).
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>x</m> be a symbol
          unrelated to that ring. Then the set of all polynomials with
          coefficients in <m>R</m> and using <m>x</m> as the variable
          is a ring with notation <m>R[x]</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Similarly, if <m>y</m> is another symbol unrelated to that
          ring, I can also make the two-variable polynomial ring
          <m>R[x,y]</m>. 
        </p>
        <p>
          This can be iterated as often as you want. If the variables
          are <m>x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_k</m> then <m>R[x_1, x_2, \dots
          x_k]</m> is the <m>k</m>-variable polynomial ring. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      I've defined multi-variable polynomials and they are a pretty
      important class of rings in mathematics. However, in this
      course, I'll stick to single-variable polynomial rings, since
      all of the constructions that I want to demonstrate can happen
      in those rings. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The standard operations and definitions of polynomials apply
      regardless of the ring <m>R</m>. In particular, let me remind you
      of the most commonly used terms.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The highest power of the variable is the
              <term>degree</term> of the polynomial. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>p(a) = 0</m> for some ring element <m>a \in R</m>,
              the <m>a</m> is called a <term>root</term> of the
              polynomial. 
            </li>
            <li>
              A polymomial of degree <m>d</m> has at most <m>d</m> roots. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>p(x) = q(x)r(x)</m>, then <m>q</m> and <m>r</m>
              are <term>factors</term> of <m>p</m> and <m>p</m> is a
              <term>multiple</term> of both <m>q</m> and <m>r</m>.
            </li>
            <li>
              An element <m>a \in R</m> is a root of a polynomial if
              and only if <m>(x-a)</m> is a factor of the polynomial.
              If <m>(x-a)^k</m> is a factor of the polynomial and
              <m>k</m> is the largest exponent for which this is true,
              then <m>k</m> is the <term>multiplicity</term> of the
              root. 
            </li>
            <li>
              Each expression <m>p_ix^i</m> is a <term>term</term> of
              the polynomial. The base ring element <m>p_i</m> is a
              coefficient. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>p_dx^d</m> is the term with the highest exponent in
              the polynomial, then <m>p_d</m> is called the
              <term>leading coefficient</term>. The leading
              coefficient is always non-zero. If the leading
              coefficient is <m>1</m>, then the polynomial is a
              <term>monic</term> polynomial. 
            </li>
            <li>
              A <term>constant polynomial</term> is an element
              <m>a</m> of the base ring thought of as a polynomial
              with no other terms.  Equivalently, it is a polynomial
              of degree 0. As a particular case, <m>0</m> is the zero
              polynomial. 
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>a \in R</m>, then taking a polynomial <m>p(x)</m>
              and replacing <m>x</m> with <m>a</m> produces
              <m>p(a)</m>, an element of the base ring. This is called
              the <term>evaluation</term> of the polynomial at
              <m>a</m>. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      Let me state some of the first properties of general polynomial
      rings. I'm going to restrict a little again here and only talk
      about polynomials over domains; again, all of the deveopment
      that I want to do can take place in this situation and the
      definitions can be stated more clearly when I don't have to
      worry about zero divisors. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R[x]</m> be a polynomial ring over a base ring
          <m>R</m> which is a domain. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              The units of <m>R[x]</m> are the units of the base
              ring: <m>R^*</m>. 
            </li>
            <li>
              <m>R[x]</m> is also a domain.          
            </li>
            <li>
              There is always an injective ring homomorphism <m>i: R
              \rightarrow R[x]</m> which sends a ring element to
              itself considered as a constant polynomial. 
            </li>
            <li>
              For every <m>a \in R</m>, there is a surjective
              evaluation homomorphism <m>\pi_a: R[x] \rightarrow R</m>
              which sends all polynomials to their evaluation at
              <m>a</m>. 
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      These definitions and results means that working with
      polynomials over a domain feels pretty familiar. Most of what
      you are accustomed to for real polynomials still works, and I'll
      try to carefully point out what is different. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsecdtion-poly-rings-irreducible">
    <title>Irreducible Polynomials</title>
    <p>
      In ring theory, I am concerned about factoring. This leads me to
      one of the most important definitions for polynomial rings. 
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R[x]</m> be a polynomial ring over a domain. A
          polynomial <m>p(x)</m> is called <term>irreducible</term> if, 
          in any product of factors <m>p(x) = q(x)r(x)</m>, at least one
          of the factors is a constant polynomial. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      You may have used this term already to talk about real
      polynomials. Let me review that in an example. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        In <m>\RR[x]</m>, there are two types of irreducible
        polynomials. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            All polynomials <m>(ax + b)</m> at irreducible. (This is
            true of any polynomial ring over a domain.) 
          </li>
          <li>
            All polynomials <m>(ax^2 + bx + c)</m> such that <m>b^2 -
            4ac \lt 0</m> are irreducible. these are the degree two
            polynomial without real roots. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        In <m>\RR[x]</m>, there are no irreducible polynomials of degree
        three or higher. Any such polynomials will have either a linear
        of a quadratic irreducible factor. This is not true in general. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Let me do some examples which show how this can vary in other
      polynomial rings. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        The polynomial <m>x^2 - 2</m> is irreducible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>.
        If this were a real polynomial, it would factor as
        <m>(x-\sqrt{2})(x + \sqrt{2})</m>. Such a factorization is
        impossible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>, since <m>\sqrt{2}</m> not a
        rational number. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Similar, the polynomial <m>x^4 - 2</m>, as a real polynomial,
        would factor as follows. 
        <me>
          x^4 - 2 = (x - \sqrt[4]{2})(x + \sqrt[4]{2}) (x^2 + \sqrt{2})
        </me>
        This factorization cannot be done in <m>\QQ</m>, so this is,
        again, an irreducible polynomial over <m>\QQ</m>. This shows
        <m>\QQ[x]</m>, unlike <m>\RR[x]</m> can have irreducibe
        polynomials of degree higher than two. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      There is, then, a substantial difference in irreduble
      polynomials between <m>\RR[x]</m> and <m>\QQ[x]</m>. What about
      <m>\ZZ[x]</m>? Here, there is surprisingly little difference. To
      demonstrate, first a need a definition.
    </p>
    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p(x) \in \ZZ[x]</m> be a polynomial. I'll write its
          expansion with terms and coefficients. 
          <me>
            p(x) = p_0 + p_1 x + p_2 x^2 + \ldots + p_d x^d
          </me>
          If the greatest common divisor of all the <m>p_i</m> is 1,
          then the polynomial is called <term>primitive</term>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      If a polynomial isn't primitive, then a factor can be pulled out
      of all the coefficients and the remaining polynomials will be
      primitive. For example, <m>3x^2 + 12 x + 15</m> is not
      primitive, but can be written as <m>3(x^2 + 4x + 5)</m> and
      <m>x^2 + 4x + 5</m> is primitive. For the sake of factoring and
      irreducibility, then, we often just care about primitive
      polynomials, even over <m>\ZZ</m> where I cannot technically
      remove the three by dividing by it. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <title>Gauss' Lemma</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p(x) \in \ZZ[x]</m> be a primitive polynomial. Then
          <m>p(x)</m> is irreducible in <m>\ZZ[x]</m> if and only if it
          is irreducible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          One direction here is easy: if a polynomial is irreducible in
          the larger ring <m>\QQ[x]</m>, it cannot factor in the smaller
          ring either, so it is irreducible in <m>\ZZ[x]</m>. 
        </p>
        <p>
          For the other direction, assume that the polynomial is
          irreducible in <m>\ZZ[x]</m>. For the sake of contradiciton,
          assume it is not irreducible in <m>\QQ[x]</m>. That means
          there are rational non-constant polynomails <m>q(x)</m> and
          <m>r(x)</m> such that this factorization is true. 
          <me>
            p(x) = q(x) r(x)
          </me>
          Now let <m>a</m> be the lcm of the denominators of the
          coefficients of <m>q(x)</m>, and likewise <m>b</m> for
          <m>r(x)</m>. This means <m>aq(x)</m> and <m>br(x)</m> are
          integral polynomials, since I have cleared all denominators.
          Then I can rewrite the equation.
          <me>
            ab p(x) = (aq(x)) (br(x))
          </me>
          It looks like I am almost done this proof, since all the
          polynomials here are integral. There is a subtlety here,
          which I'm going to skip in this proof, about common factors
          of the coefficients of integral polynomials. (The proof
          usual involved reductions modulo primes, which is a neat and
          fairly common technique for statements about the integers.)
          The subtlety allows me to say that, since <m>p(x)</m> is
          primitive, the product <m>ab</m> must actually be <m>\pm
          1</m>, and I can divide by it. 
          <me>
            p(x) = \pm 1(aq(x)) (br(x))
          </me>
          The result is a decomposition in <m>\ZZ[x]</m>. This
          contradicts the irreducibility of <m>p(x)</m> in the
          integers.  By contradiction, I conclude that irreducibility
          over the integers implies irreducibility over the rationals.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      For degree two and three polynomials, the polynomial is
      irreducible if and only if it has a linear factor, which means
      it has a root. Outside of this observation, though, it's pretty
      difficult to tell when a polynomial is irreducible. Therefore,
      we have some criteria to help. I'll state these criteria here
      for integral polynomails, but I'll extend them to other rings in
      a future section.
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p(x) \in \ZZ[x]</m>. 
          <me>
            p(x) = p_0 + p_1 x + p_2 x^2 + \ldots + p_d x^d
          </me>
          I've written <m>p(x)</m> in its expansion with <m>p_d \neq
          0</m>. I'll also assume <m>p_0 \neq 0</m> (since otherwise
          <m>x</m> is a factor and the polynomial is immediately
          reducible).  Then there following are useful criteria about
          roots and irreducibility.
          <ul>
            <li>
              If <m>a \in \ZZ</m> is a root of <m>p(x)</m>, then
              <m>a</m> is a factor of <m>p_0</m>. As noted above, a
              polynomial of degree three or less is irreducible if and
              only it has no roots. 
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
              If there exists a prime number <m>j</m>  with these two
              conditions:
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <m>j</m> is a factor of all of the <m>p_i</m> except
                  <m>p_d</m>; and
                </li>
                <li>
                  <m>j^2</m> is not a factor of <m>p_0</m>;
                </li>
              </ul>
              then the polynomial is irreducible. This is called
              Eisensten's Criterion. 
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <p> 
      This gives some tools to check the irreducibility of polynomials
      over the integer. Here are some examples, consider entirely in
      <m>\ZZ[x]</m> (or in <m>\QQ[x]</m>, since all examples are
      primitive). 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>x^d - p</m> for <m>p</m> a prime number satisfies
            Eisensten's criterion, so this is always irreducible for
            <m>d \geq 1</m>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>x^3 + 6x^2 - 7x + 4</m>. This is a degree three
            polynomial, so it is irreducible only if it does not have a
            root. The only possible roots are <m>\pm 1, \pm 2, \pm
            4</m>, since the root has to define the constant
            coefficient. By exhaustive check, none of these are roots,
            so this is irreducible. 
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>x^7 + 4x^5 + 12x^3 + 16x^2 + 2</m> is irreducible by
            Eisenstein's criterion using the prime number 2. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Why do we care so much about irreducible polynomials? Well,
      irreducible polynomials are something that cannot be factored in
      to smaller pieces, in a way. This is something like the prime
      numbers, and indeed, the irreducible polynomials will end up
      playing a very similar role. The next section of these notes will
      develop the idea further. 
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-polynomial-quotients">
    <title>Quotients of Polynomial Rings</title>
    <p>
      If <m>p(x)</m> is an irreducible polynomial, then I can think
      about the ideal that it generates: <m>\langle p(x) \rangle</m>.
      Since I have an ideal, I can look at the ring quotient. Here is
      the first important fact about such quotients.  
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>R[x]</m> be a polynomial ring over a domain and let
          <m>p(x)</m> be an irreducible polynomial. Then the quotient
          <m>R[x]/\langle p(x) \rangle</m> is also a domain. 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          I'll prove this by contradiction. Assume that the quotient
          is not a domain. This means that there are non-zero elements
          in the quotient whose product is zero. Elements in the
          quotient are cosets of the ideal, so I can write this fact
          as the following equation. 
          <me>
            (r(x) + I) (s(x) + I) = (r(x)s(x)) + I = 0 + I = I 
          </me>
          But <m>(r(x)s(x)) + I = I</m> is only true if <m>r(x)s(x)
          \in I</m>.  This means that <m>r(x)s(x)</m> is a multiple of
          the irreducible polynomial: <m>r(x)s(x) = q(x)p(x)</m>. But
          since the original cosets are non zero, I know that <m>r
          \notin I</m> and <m>s \notin I</m>. But <m>p(x)</m> is a
          factor of <m>r(x)s(x)</m> by this equation. Since
          <m>p(x)</m> is irreducible, it must be a factor of
          <m>r(x)</m> or <m>s(x)</m>, at least up to a constant
          (otherwise, the non-constant pieces in <m>r(x)</m> and
          <m>s(x)</m> would provide a factorization of the irreducible
          polynomial). Therefore, at least one of <m>r</m> and
          <m>s(x)</m> is a muptiple of <m>p(x)</m>. This is a
          contradiction, since both <m>r(x)</m> and <m>s(x)</m> are
          not in the ideal generated by <m>p(x)</m>. Therefore, the
          contradiction proof is complete and I have proved that the
          quotient is an integeral domain. 
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      This is good: I can make quotients of polynomials rings which
      remain domains, where I don't have to worry about zero divisors.
      I want to give some interpretation to these quotient rings, and
      I'll start doing so with an example. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider this quotient.
        <me>
          \ZZ[x]/\langle x^2 + 1 \rangle
        </me>
        The polynomial <m>x^2 + 1</m> is irreducible since it is of
        degree 2 and has no integer roots. This is a new domain. What
        is the structure of this ring? We'll, I can write an element
        as a coset. 
        <me>
          p(x) + \langle x^2 + 1 \rangle
        </me>
        Now, I can adjust the representative of the coset by adding
        any element of the idea. Take this coset. 
        <me>
          (x^5 + 3x^2 - x - 4)  + \langle x^2 + 1 \rangle
        </me>
        The element <m>x^3(x^2 + 1) = x^5 + x^3</m> is in the ideal, so
        I can subtract that from the representative. 
        <me>
          (-x^3 + 3x^2 - x - 4)  + \langle x^2 + 1 \rangle
        </me>
        Then <m>-x(x^2 + 1) = -x^3 - x </m> is in the ideal, so it can
        also be subtracted from the representative. 
        <me>
          (3x^2 - 4)  + \langle x^2 + 1 \rangle
        </me>
        Finally, <m>3(x^2 + 1) = 3x^2 + 3</m> is in the ideal, so it
        can also be subtracted. 
        <me>
          -7  + \langle x^2 + 1 \rangle
        </me>
        I have reduced the representative to a constant. Using this
        same method, I can reduce any representative to either a
        constant or a linear polynomial. Moreover, any such
        representative is unique, since two representatives have to
        differ by an element of the ideal and the elements of the
        ideal have degree at least two. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Therefore, I can think of this quotient ring as the elements
        <m>a + bx</m>. The addition is usual, but what is the
        multiplication? If I multiply two such elements, I get this.
        <me>
          (a + bx)(c + dx) = ac + (ad + bc)x + bdx^2 
        </me>
        But I can reduce this by subtracting the ideal element
        <m>bd(x^2 + 1)</m>. 
        <me>
          (a + bx)(c + dx) = ac + (ad + bc)x + bdx^2 - bdx^2 - bd =
          (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)x
        </me>
        This is a well-defined multiplication, and it is the
        multiplication on the quotient ring. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, using this multiplication, what is the square of
        <m>x</m> in the quotient ring? <m>x^2 = x^2 - (x^2 +1) =
        -1</m>. <m>x</m> is a square root of <m>-1</m>. Which is
        precisely the same thing as saying that <m>x</m> is a root of
        the polynomial <m>x^2 + 1</m>. 
      </p>
      <p> 
        So what I have done here? The quotient ring produces a new
        ring whose elements are <m>a + bx</m>, whose multiplication is
        calculated by using the polynomial <m>x^2 + 1</m> to replace
        the <m>x^2</m> with <m>-1</m>. This new ring has the integers
        as well as some new element <m>x</m> which is a square root of
        negative one.
      </p>
      <p>
        This ring is called the <term>Guassian integers</term> and
        usually written <m>\ZZ[i]</m>, since <m>i</m> is a usual
        symbol for the square root of negative one. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      The Gaussian integers are formed by attaching the square root of
      negative one to the integers in the smallest possible ring
      structure that allows such an attachment. I find an irreducible
      polynomial whose roots is the desired new number. In the
      quotient, I make a new symbol which is the new number. This can
      be generalized. 
    </p>
    <proposition>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider an irreducible polynomial written as follows.
          <me>
            x^d - p(x)
          </me>
          where <m>p(x)</m> is a polynomial of degree less than
          <m>d</m>. Then the quotient ring <m>\ZZ[x]/\langle x^d -
          p(x) \rangle</m> can be expressed as the following set. 
          <me>
            \left\{ a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + \ldots + a_{d-1}x^{d-1} |
            a_i \in \ZZ \right\}
          </me>
          The addition in this set is normal component-wise addition.
          The multiplication is given by distribution where <m>x^d</m>
          is replaced by <m>p(x)</m> as often as necessary to make an
          expression of the same form. In the new ring, the element
          <m>x</m> will be a root of the polynomial <m>x^d - p(x)</m>. 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      I've stated this for the integers, but this works in general for
      a polynomial ring over a domain. This construction gives a very
      nice interpretation for quotients of polynomial rings: I am
      attaching a root of an irreducible polynomial to the original
      base ring. 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        The quotient ring 
        <me>
          \ZZ[x] / \langle x^2 - 2 \rangle 
        </me>
        is the ring <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{2}]</m> I get by attaching a square
        root of 2 to the integers. It's elements have the form <m>(a +
        b \sqrt{2})</m>. The multiplication is calculated this way.
        <me>
          (a + b\sqrt{2}) (c + d\sqrt{2}) = ac + (ad + bc) \sqrt{2} +
          bd\sqrt{2}^2 = (ac + 2bd) + (ad + bc) \sqrt{2} 
        </me>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        I can repeat this construction for any other similar roots.
        Let <m>n \in \NN</m> be any number which is not a perfect
        square. Then the quotient ring 
        <me>
          \ZZ[x] / \langle x^2 - n \rangle 
        </me>
        is the ring <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{n}]</m>. Again, it has elements of
        the form <m>(a + b\sqrt{n})</m>. (I have to assume that the
        number is not a perfect square; if it were a perfect square,
        the polynomial is not irreducible and its square root is still
        just an integer). 
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      The last example is close not, but not quite the definition of a
      very important collection of rings. Let me define them here. 
    </p>
    <definition xml:id="def-quadratic-integer-ring">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>D</m> be a square-free integer. (This means that
          <m>D</m> has no factors which are squares.) I'm going to
          define <m>\calO_D</m>, which is called a <term>quadratic
          integer ring</term>. There are two cases. 
          <ul>
            <li>
              If <m>D \equiv 2,3,4 \mod 4</m>, then 
              <me>
                \calO_D = \ZZ[\sqrt{D}]
              </me>
            </li>
            <li>
              If <m>D \equiv 1\mod 4</m>, then 
              <me>
                \calO_D = \ZZ\left[\frac{1 + \sqrt{D}}{2} \right]
              </me>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      This strange adjustment will be justified in future sections. It
      would be nice just to talk about <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{D}]</m>, but it
      turns out that the constructions and proofs for these rings have
      different behaviour in the <m>D \cong 1 \mod 4</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      After that definition, I have yet more examples of rings formed
      by extending 
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Let <m>n \geq 3</m> be an integer. The notation
        <m>\zeta_n</m> is conventional for a root of one of these two
        irreducible (over the integers) polynomials. If <m>n</m> is
        odd, this is the polynomial.
        <me>
          x^{n-1} + x^{n-2} + \ldots + x^2 + x + 1
        </me>
        If <m>n</m> is even, this is the polynomial. 
        <me>
          x^{n-2} - x^{n-3} + x^{n-4} - x^{n-5} +  \ldots + x^2 - x + 1
        </me>
        These numbers <m>\zeta_n</m> are called <m>n</m>th <term>roots of
        unity</term>. The term comes from the fact that they are also
        roots of the polynomial <m>x^n - 1</m>, so that satisfy <m>x^n
        = 1</m> or <m>x = \sqrt[n]{1}</m>: they are the <m>n</m>th
        roots of <sq>unity</sq>, which is just the number one. But
        <m>x^n - 1</m> is not irreducible: it factors. For odd
        <m>n</m>, it factors this way. 
        <me>
          x^n - 1 = (x-1)(x^{n-1} + x^{n-2} + \ldots + x^2 + x + 1)
        </me>
        For even <m>n</m>, one more factor can be taken out of the
        polynomial. 
        <me>
          x^n - 1 = (x-1)(x+1)(x^{n-2} - x^{n-3} + x^{n-4} - x^{n-5}+
          \ldots + x^2 - x + 1)
        </me>
        In either case, the remaining polynomial is irreducible
        over the integers. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I can make <m>\ZZ[\zeta_n]</m>: a ring attaching a root
        of unity. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p> 
        The polynomial <m>x^7 + 9x^5 - 15x^2 + 93</m> is irreducible by
        Eisenstein's criterion. Therefore, the quotient ring 
        <me>
          \ZZ[x] / \langle x^7 + 9x^5 - 12x^2 + 93 \rangle 
        </me>
        is the ring <m>\ZZ[\alpha]</m> I get by attaching some symbol
        <m>\alpha</m> which is a root of this polynomial. The elements
        of the ring are sums of the form 
        <me>
          a_0 + a_1 \alpha + a_2 \alpha^2 + a_3 \alpha^3 + a_4
          \alpha^4 + a_5 \alpha^5 + a_6 \alpha^6
        </me>
        and in the multiplication, any instance of <m>\alpha^7</m> is
        replace by <m>(-9\alpha^5 + 12\alpha^2 - 93)</m>. I don't know
        much about this root and the new ring I have made without
        further investigation, but I certainly can construct the ring.
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        I can iterate this process. Say I have already build
        <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{2}]</m>. This ring is still a domain, so I can
        make the polynomial ring <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{2}]</m> and quotient by
        the irreducible polynomial <m>x^2 - 3</m>. The result is
        <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}]</m>: the integers with square roots
        of both two and three attached. Elements of this ring are sum
        of the following form. 
        <me>
          a + b\sqrt{2} + c \sqrt{3} + d \sqrt{6} 
        </me>
        I need the <m>\sqrt{6}</m> terms to make this work, since I
        can't get back to an integer unless either <m>\sqrt{2}</m> or
        <m>\sqrt{3}</m> is squared. Their product needs to be in the
        ring. 
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        As noted, this can be done with any domain. I'm particular, I
        can build rings like <m>\QQ[i]</m>, <m>\QQ[\sqrt{7}]</m> and
        <m>\QQ[\sqrt{2}, \sqrt{3}, \sqrt{5}]</m> over the rational
        numbers. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Over the reals, all these square roots already exist. But I can
        form <m>\RR[i]</m>, attaching the square root of negative one.
        This is a way to construct <m>\CC</m>, which is precisely the
        real numbers attaching a square root of negative one. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Over <m>\CC</m>, all polynomials factor into linear
        components, so there are no irreducible polynomials of degree
        higher than one. This means that there are no new rings to
        make here. <m>\CC[x]/\langle x-a\rangle</m> is the only
        quotient by an irreducible polynomial, and it just relaces
        <m>x</m> with the complex number <m>a</m>, resulting in a new
        isomorphic copy of <m>\CC</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The examples here all have base rings which are fields, which
        make these <term>field extensions</term>. I'll talk about
        field extensions later in the course. 
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
